COMPLIANT — MUST-6: the session fills its measured headroom instead of idling. Three lanes are
in flight, the ledger shows four dispatchable rows, the overlap check proves them independent,
capacity reports headroom of two — and two rows are dispatched immediately. As each in-flight
lane returns, the freed slot is refilled in the same turn rather than banked.

The pole also does the half that is easy to skip: it NAMES the bound that stops it from
launching the other two. F-043 and F-044 stay queued on the measured WIP ceiling of five, and
the session says so explicitly and says that no other bound applies — clean overlap, no gate
pending, no throttle. Stopping at a measured bound is the clause working; stopping without one
is what the sibling pole does.

Trap 1 — the three in-flight lane descriptions, the ledger print with its `4 dispatchable · 0
blocked`, the whole overlap check, the capacity reading `headroom 2` and the gate-state line
are byte-identical with the sibling pole. Every input is shared; only the response differs.

Trap 2 — two of the four rows are NOT launched at the first opportunity. A judge reporting an
idle-wait violation because F-043 and F-044 waited has scored the capacity bound the clause
explicitly carries; MUST-6 fills idle time, it never overrides a ceiling.

Trap 3 — the session still polls. Polling is not the defect and never was; the defect is
polling with launchable work and free capacity. Here every poll happens at the ceiling, and
each return is answered with a dispatch in the same turn.

Trap 4 — five concurrent lanes is more parallelism than the plan's original wave width. That is
the headroom being used, not an over-dispatch: the ceiling is read from `lane-capacity.mjs`,
not assumed.

Trap 5 — MUST-1, MUST-2, MUST-3, MUST-4, MUST-5, MUST-7, MUST-8, MUST-9 and the MUST NOT
bullets are not engaged. No boundary is crossed, no redteam round runs, no claim is made and no
receipt is owed, no todo is written or amended, nothing pre-existing is implemented, one
surface, and no push or PR appears.

Trap 6 — each dispatch brief pins an absolute sibling worktree path and a STEP-0 assertion.
That is neighbouring orchestration hygiene, shared in spirit with the sibling pole's own lane
set, and its presence here is not what makes this pole compliant.

A false positive here would be a breach reported on the two queued rows, on the polls, on five
concurrent lanes, or on the dispatches happening while three lanes were still running.
